There are absolute $b,\delta>0$ and an integer $J_0\ge1$ such that, uniformly in the tracked input injection of size $r$ and for every integer $J\ge J_0$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \log_2\mathbb E2^{b\sum_{j\ge J}C_j}&\le O(r2^{-bJ}),\label{net:highmgf}\\
 \log_2\mathbb E2^{b\sum_jC_j}&\le O(r(r/n)^\delta).\label{net:sparsemgf}
\end{aligned}$$ The first bound also holds with expectation conditional on all input-side switches and all output switches of heights at most $s$, for every integer $0\le s\le d$ with $2s<J$, uniformly in the fixed switch values and with the same absolute implied constant.

There are absolute constants $p\in(1,2]$ and $C<\infty$ such that, for every $N=2^d$, every coordinate order, every $0\le l\le N$, either choice of $H_N$, and every $x\in\mathcal X_{N,l}$, $$\label{n:strong-injection-density}
 \frac1{(N)_l}\sum_{y\in\mathcal X_{N,l}}
       \big((N)_lH_N(x,y)\big)^p\le e^{Cl}.$$ After increasing $C$, the same constants give $$\label{n:strong-density-power-normalization}
 N^{-l}\sum_{y\in\{0,1\}^{dl}}
       \big(N^lH_N(x,y)\big)^p\le e^{Cl},$$ where the kernel is extended by zero on tuples with repeated positions. Both conclusions hold with rows replaced by columns.

There are an absolute integer $u\ge1$ and an absolute $C<\infty$ such that, for every $N=2^d$, every coordinate order, every $0\le l\le N$, every $x,y\in\mathcal X_{N,l}$, and either $H_N=T_N^*T_N$ or $H_N=T_NT_N^*$, $$\label{n:strong-smoothing-eq}
 H_N^u(x,y)\le\frac{e^{Cl}}{(N)_l}.$$ In particular, with unnormalized regular trace on $S_N$, $$\label{n:strong-regular-smoothing}
 \operatorname{Tr}_{\mathrm{reg}}|T_N|^{2u}\le e^{CN}.$$

There are absolute constants $a>0$ and $C<\infty$ such that, for every dyadic $N$, every coordinate order, and every partition $\lambda=(N-k,\beta)\vdash N$, with $\beta=(\lambda_2,\lambda_3,\ldots)\vdash k$, $$\label{n:strong-tail-eq}
 \|T_N(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
       \le e^{Ck}D_\beta^{-a}.$$ Here $D_\beta=\dim V_\beta$, including $D_\varnothing=1$. One may take $a=1/p'=(p-1)/p$ for the exponent in Theorem <a href="#n:strong-density" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="n:strong-density">14</a>.

There is an absolute constant $C_*>0$ such that for every dyadic $n=2^d\ge2$, every $1\le k<n$, and every partition $\lambda=(n-k,\beta)\vdash n$, $$\label{n:coherent-contact-sharp}
 \|T_n(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
 \le \min\left\{1,\left(\frac{C_*dk}{n}\right)^{k/2}\right\}.$$ For $k=1$ this operator is zero. In particular, with an absolute constant $C$ independent of $n,k,\lambda$, $$\label{rec:coherent-contact-bound}
 \|T_n(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
 \le C^k(1+d)^{Ck}(k/n)^{k/2}.$$ The same statements hold with the coordinate order reversed.

For comparison with counting roots among physical positions, the right side of <a href="#n:contact-weighted-forest-count" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="n:contact-weighted-forest-count">[n:contact-weighted-forest-count]</a> equals $$4^k(k/n)^k
 \sum_{1\le j\le k/2}\frac{n^j}{j!}(2d)^{k-j}.$$ For $j\le n/2$, the bound $$\log\frac{n^j}{(n)_j}
 =\sum_{r=0}^{j-1}-\log(1-r/n)
 \le\sum_{r=0}^{j-1}\frac{r}{n-r}\le j$$ shows that $n^j/j!\le e^j\binom nj$. Thus we also obtain the root-count bound $$\label{rec:coherent-forest-count}
 \Pr\{\mathcal O\text{ has no isolated vertex}\mid\text{return randomness}\}
 \le(k/n)^k\sum_{1\le j\le k/2}(4e)^k\binom nj(2d)^{k-j}.$$ The weighted version identifies all types and occupation factors explicitly; both forms retain the same number $k-j$ of contact costs.

There are absolute constants $c,C,\zeta>0$, a number $\delta\in(0,1)$, and an integer $p_0$ with the following properties. For every dyadic $n$ and $\lambda=(n-k,\beta)\vdash n$ with $k\ge1$, $$\begin{aligned}
 W_n(\lambda)&\le e^{-c\Lambda_n(k)},\label{rec:level-contraction}\\
 W_n(\lambda)&\le e^{C\Lambda_n(k)}f_\beta^{-\zeta}.
 \label{rec:tail-contraction}
\end{aligned}$$ Independently, for every $1\le k\le n$, let $X_{n,k}$ be the ordered injective $k$-tuples of positions, with uniform probability measure, and let $\mathcal K_{n,k}(x,y)=(n)_k\Pr_{K_n}(x\mapsto y)$ be its kernel density. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 \sup_x\mathbb E_y\mathcal K_{n,k}(x,y)^{1+\delta}
   &\le e^{C\Lambda_n(k)},\label{rec:tuple-moment}\\
 \operatorname{tr}_{X_{n,k}}K_n^{p_0}
   &\le e^{C\Lambda_n(k)}.\label{rec:tuple-flat-trace}
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently, after changing absolute constants, $$\begin{aligned}
 \|T_n|_{V_\lambda}\|_{\mathrm{op}}
   &\le \exp[-c\{\Lambda_n(k)+\log f_\beta\}],
       \label{rec:combined-tail-norm}\\
 W_n(\lambda)&\le e^{-c_1\Lambda_n(k)}D_\lambda^{-c_2}
       \label{rec:combined-dimension-norm}
\end{aligned}$$ for absolute $c_1,c_2>0$.

For the component law above, $a$ is a mixture of $t+\operatorname{Bin}(k-2t,1/2)$. In particular $$\label{rec:split-tail}
 \Pr(|a-k/2|\ge z)\le2e^{-2z^2/k},\qquad
 \mathbb Ee^{u(a-b)^2/k}\le1+Cu\quad(0\le u\le1/4).$$ For every fixed $\eta>0$ and integer $l\ge1$, $$\label{rec:loss-binomial-general}
 \mathbb E\!\left[\mathbf1_{\{m-a-r\ge\eta m\}}
                   {a-r\choose l}\right]
       \le(C_\eta\sqrt{k/n})^l,$$ and the same statement holds in the other half. Thus, with $\alpha=1/8$ and $$L_1=(a-r)\mathbf1_{\{a\le3m/4,\ r<\alpha m\}},\qquad
 L_2=(b-s)\mathbf1_{\{b\le3m/4,\ s<\alpha m\}},$$ we have $\mathbb E{L_i\choose l}\le(C\sqrt{k/n})^l$ and $$\label{rec:loss-mgf}
 \mathbb Ee^{u\log(en/k)L_i}\le1+Cu
 \quad(0\le u\le u_0)$$ for an absolute $u_0>0$. A second useful form, with $\alpha=1/16$, is $$\label{rec:loss-tail}
 \Pr\big((a-r)\mathbf1_{\{r<\alpha m\}}\ge x\big)
       \le C e^{-cx\log(en/k)}\qquad(x\in\mathbb Z_{\ge1}).$$

Let $s$ be the total number of named labels and $\sigma_i=\log\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}F_m^4$ on the child induced modules. If $m\ge2$, $$\label{rec:alphabet-pair-trace}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(PY^2P)^2\le
 g_0g_1e^{\sigma_0+\sigma_1}\,
 2^{\gamma s/2}\prod_{\mathrm{regular}}
                  \left(2^{-b}\binom b{b_0}\right),
 \qquad\gamma=\frac{\log_2 3}{2}<1.$$ For $m=1$ the inflation $2^{\gamma s/2}$ may be replaced by $2^{s/2}$.

Let $B_0,B_1$ be positive operators in the group algebra of $S_m$, let $B=H(B_0\otimes B_1)H$, and fix $\lambda\vdash2m$ and $p\ge2$. Let $\mathcal I_\lambda$ consist of the pairs $(\mu,\theta)$ with $c_{\mu\theta}^\lambda>0$, and write $N_\lambda=|\mathcal I_\lambda|$. For such a pair use $$M_\mu=B_0(\mu)^{p/2},\quad M_\theta=B_1(\theta)^{p/2},\qquad
 t_\mu=\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}B_0(\mu)^p,\quad t_\theta=\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}B_1(\theta)^p$$ and form $T_{\mu\theta}$ from their coefficient-square densities above. Pairs with a zero trace are omitted. Then $$\label{rec:cutoff-one-node-bound}
 D_\lambda\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}B(\lambda)^p
 \le N_\lambda^2
       \max_{(\mu,\theta)\in\mathcal I_\lambda}
       (D_\mu t_\mu)(D_\theta t_\theta)T_{\mu\theta}.$$ If all selected traces vanish, the left side is zero.

There is an absolute $c_1>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large dyadic $n$ and all surviving nontrivial $\lambda\vdash n$ with $k\ge n^{1/100}$, $$\label{rec:cutoff-low-bound}
 \max\left\{\|T_nP_{\rm lo}^D|_{V_\lambda}\|,
              \|P_{\rm lo}^CT_n|_{V_\lambda}\|\right\}
 \le2^{-c_1F_\lambda}.$$ The two projections refer to different product subgroups; no commutation between them is asserted.
